./contributingGuides/STYLE.md:## Onyx Best Practices
./contributingGuides/STYLE.md:[Onyx Documentation](https://github.com/expensify/react-native-onyx)
./contributingGuides/STYLE.md:**Exception:** There are some [gotchas](https://github.com/expensify/react-native-onyx#merging-data) when working with complex nested array values in Onyx. So, this could be another valid reason to break a property off of its parent object (e.g. `reportActions` are easier to work with as a separate collection).
./contributingGuides/FORMS.md:Form inputs will NOT store draft values by default. This is to avoid accidentally storing any sensitive information like passwords, SSN or bank account information. We need to explicitly tell each form input to save draft values by passing the `shouldSaveDraft` prop to the input. Saving draft values is highly desirable and we should always try to save draft values. This way when a user continues a given flow they can easily pick up right where they left off if they accidentally exited a flow. Inputs with saved draft values [will be cleared when a user logs out](https://github.com/Expensify/App/blob/aa1f0f34eeba5d761657168255a1ae9aebdbd95e/src/libs/actions/SignInRedirect.js#L52) (like most data). Additionally, we should clear draft data once the form is successfully submitted by calling `Onyx.set(ONYXKEY.FORM_ID, null)` in the onSubmit callback passed to Form.
./contributingGuides/OFFLINE_UX.md:- or data is READ from the server and does not need to show up-to-date data. The user will see stale data until the new data is put into Onyx and then the view updates to show the new data.
./contributingGuides/API.md:- Data is pushed to the client and put straight into Onyx by low-level libraries.
./contributingGuides/API.md:    The network library will look for any `onyxData` in the response and send it straight to `Onyx.update(response.onyxData)`.
./contributingGuides/API.md:    Pusher listens for an `onyxApiUpdate` event and sends the data straight to `Onyx.update(pushJSON)`.
./contributingGuides/API.md:Error messages should be returned and stored as an object under the `errors` property, keyed by an integer [microtime](https://github.com/Expensify/Web-Expensify/blob/25d056c9c531ea7f12c9bf3283ec554dd5d1d316/lib/Onyx.php#L148-L154). It's also common to store errors keyed by microtime under `errorFields.fieldName`. Use this format when error messages should be saved on a general object but are only relevant to a specific field / key on the object. If absolutely needed, additional error properties can be stored under other, more specific fields that sit at the same level as `errors`:
./contributingGuides/API.md:    'onyxMethod' => Onyx::METHOD_MERGE,
./contributingGuides/API.md:    'key' => OnyxKeys::WALLET_ADDITIONAL_DETAILS,
./contributingGuides/API.md:            Onyx::getErrorMicroTime() => 'We\'re having trouble verifying your SSN. Please enter the full 9 digits of your SSN.',
./contributingGuides/API.md:### How should error messages be persisted to Onyx?
./contributingGuides/API.md:Use `Onyx.set(key, null)` for each data (eg. policy) not found in the server.
