./README.md:To make it easier to test things in web, we expose the Onyx object to the window, so you can easily do `Onyx.set('bla', 1)`.
./README.md:## Onyx
./README.md:- Onyx stores and retrieves data from persistent storage
./README.md:- Onyx allows other code to subscribe to changes in data, and then publishes change events whenever data is changed
./README.md:- Anything needing to read Onyx data needs to:
./README.md:    1. Know what key the data is stored in (for web, you can find this by looking in the JS console > Application > IndexedDB > OnyxDB > keyvaluepairs)
./README.md:    2. Subscribe to changes of the data for a particular key or set of keys. React components use `withOnyx()` and non-React libs use `Onyx.connect()`
./README.md:    3. Get initialized with the current value of that key from persistent storage (Onyx does this by calling `setState()` or triggering the `callback` with the values currently on disk as part of the connection process)
./README.md:- Subscribing to Onyx keys is done using a constant defined in `ONYXKEYS`. Each Onyx key represents either a collection of items or a specific entry in storage. For example, since all reports are stored as individual keys like `report_1234`, if code needs to know about all the reports (eg. display a list of them in the nav menu), then it would subscribe to the key `ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT`.
./README.md:- Subscribing to Pusher events to receive data from the server that will get put immediately into Onyx
./README.md:- Making XHRs to request necessary data from the server and then immediately putting that data into Onyx
./README.md:- Reflecting exactly the data that is in persistent storage by using `withOnyx()` to bind to Onyx data.
./README.md:As a convention, the UI layer should never interact with device storage directly or call `Onyx.set()` or `Onyx.merge()`. Use an action! For example, check out this action that is signing in the user [here](https://github.com/Expensify/App/blob/919c890cc391ad38b670ca1b266c114c8b3c3285/src/pages/signin/PasswordForm.js#L78-L78).
./README.md:That action will then call `Onyx.merge()` to [set default data and a loading state, then make an API request, and set the response with another `Onyx.merge()`](https://github.com/Expensify/App/blob/919c890cc391ad38b670ca1b266c114c8b3c3285/src/libs/actions/Session.js#L228-L247).
./README.md:    Onyx.merge(ONYXKEYS.ACCOUNT, {isLoading: true});
./README.md:            Onyx.merge(ONYXKEYS.SESSION, {authToken: response.authToken});
./README.md:            Onyx.merge(ONYXKEYS.ACCOUNT, {error: error.message});
./README.md:            Onyx.merge(ONYXKEYS.ACCOUNT, {isLoading: false});
./README.md:Keeping our `Onyx.merge()` out of the view layer and in actions helps organize things as all interactions with device storage and API handling happen in the same place. In addition, actions that are called from inside views should not ever use the `.then()` method to set loading/error states, navigate or do any additional data processing. All of this stuff should ideally go into `Onyx` and be fed back to the component via `withOnyx()`. Design your actions so they clearly describe what they will do and encapsulate all their logic in that action.
./README.md:- [Higher-Order Components](https://reactjs.org/docs/higher-order-components.html) (HOCs) should be named in camelCase, like `withOnyx`.
./README.md:Different platforms come with varying storage capacities and Onyx has a way to gracefully fail when those storage limits are encountered. When Onyx fails to set or modify a key the following steps are taken:
./README.md:1. Onyx looks at a list of recently accessed keys (access is defined as subscribed to or modified) and locates the key that was least recently accessed
./README.md:By default, Onyx will not evict anything from storage and will presume all keys are "unsafe" to remove unless explicitly told otherwise.
./README.md:- Add the key to the `safeEvictionKeys` option in `Onyx.init(options)`
./README.md:- Implement `canEvict` in the Onyx config for each component subscribing to a key
