./README.md:    1. Server pushes data to the disk of any client (Server -> Pusher event -> Action listening to pusher event -> Onyx).
./README.md:    >**Note:** Currently the code only does this with report comments. Until we make more server changes, this step is actually done by the client requesting data from the server via XHR and then storing the response in Onyx.
./README.md:    2. Disk pushes data to the UI (Onyx -> withOnyx() -> React component).
./README.md:    - Onyx is a Pub/Sub library to connect the application to the data stored on disk.
./README.md:    - UI components subscribe to Onyx (using `withOnyx()`) and any change to the Onyx data is published to the component by calling `setState()` with the changed data.
./README.md:    - Libraries subscribe to Onyx (with `Onyx.connect()`) and any change to the Onyx data is published to the callback with the changed data.
./README.md:    - The UI should never call any Onyx methods except for `Onyx.connect()`. That is the job of Actions (see next section).
./README.md:        - Incomplete or missing data. Always assume data is incomplete or not there. For example, when a comment is pushed to the client from a pusher event, it's possible that Onyx does not have data for that report yet. That's OK. A partial report object is added to Onyx for the report key `report_1234 = {reportID: 1234, isUnread: true}`. Then there is code that monitors Onyx for reports with incomplete data, and calls `openReport(1234)` to get the full data for that report. The UI should be able to gracefully handle the report object not being complete. In this example, the sidebar wouldn't display any report that does not have a report name.
./README.md:1. **Actions manage Onyx Data**
./README.md:    - Actions should favor using `Onyx.merge()` over `Onyx.set()` so that other values in an object aren't completely overwritten.
./README.md:    - Views should not call `Onyx.merge()` or `Onyx.set()` directly and should call an action instead.
./README.md:    - In general, the operations that happen inside an action should be done in parallel and not in sequence (eg. don't use the promise of one Onyx method to trigger a second Onyx method). Onyx is built so that every operation is done in parallel and it doesn't matter what order they finish in. XHRs on the other hand need to be handled in sequence with promise chains in order to access and act upon the response.
./README.md:    - If an Action needs to access data stored on disk, use a local variable and `Onyx.connect()`
./README.md:It will abstract most of the logic you need (mostly subscribe to the [NVP_PREFERRED_LOCALE](https://github.com/Expensify/App/blob/6cf1a56df670a11bf61aa67eeb64c1f87161dea1/src/ONYXKEYS.js#L88) Onyx key)
